    {
      "id": "what",
      "title": "What the number is",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "GDP per person divides a country's total output by its population. It does not say how income is shared, what things cost, or how much work happens outside the market.",
              "editorial": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "It is a starting point for asking questions, not a verdict on any place.",
              "editorial": true
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": [
    "GDP per capita in current US dollars depends on exchange rates, so it can move without anyone's life changing. Other measures, such as purchasing-power comparisons, give a different spread and are not used here.",
    "The summary figures are our own arithmetic on one year's World Bank values; a different year would change them.",
    "Nothing here covers inequality inside countries, which can be as wide as the gap between them."
  ],
  "keyPoints": [
    {
      "text": "In 2023 income per person ran from about $250 in Burundi to about $18,131 in Seychelles.",
